    {
      "type": "proposition",
      "content": "Note also from Equation 6.1 that, while inbreeding does change the genotype frequencies in a population, it does not change the allele frequencies. This is true because, for any value of F, the allele frequency of A is given by $ [p^2 + pqF] \\div (\\frac{1}{2})[2pq - 2pqF] = p^2 + pq = p(p + q) = p $. This principle requires the assumption that all genotypes have the same fitness, which is to say that no natural selection takes place. If there is selection, then the allele frequencies can change with inbreeding.",
      "source_page": 277,
      "source_pages": [
        277
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "PopGen_chapter6:p007:b3"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
